  "reasoning": "The patient presents with intense shivering/chills, a high fever, and skin manifestations including rashes on the arms and red patches on the neck. While malaria and typhoid commonly feature high fevers and chills, they do not typically present with prominent rashes on the extremities and neck. A drug reaction can cause fever and rash but usually requires a medication trigger and lacks the characteristic intense, cyclical shivering pattern. Dengue fever classically presents with a sudden onset of high fever, severe chills/shivering, and a distinctive maculopapular rash that frequently begins on the limbs and spreads to the trunk and neck. This precise combination of systemic and dermatological symptoms strongly establishes dengue as the correct diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}

